# Tango visual system

- Treat typography, photography, whitespace, scale, rhythm and motion as first-class design material.
- Avoid generic UI motifs: default dashboards, excessive cards, uniform 3-column grids, pill-everything, giant gradient blobs and stock-AI aesthetics.
- Prefer editorial compositions, asymmetry, layered depth, strong type hierarchy, tactile borders and intentional cropping.
- Use a restrained palette with a dark ink base, warm paper/ivory support and a controlled tango accent; do not spread accent color everywhere.
- Use one expressive display face plus one highly legible sans/serif text family. Avoid more than 3 font families.
- Motion must reinforce rhythm, arrival, emphasis, navigation or choreography. Never animate for decoration alone.
- Provide reduced-motion behavior for all non-essential animations.
- Design mobile as a deliberate composition, not a squeezed desktop.
- Every section should have a clear reason to exist.
- Visual variants must differ in composition, typography, imagery treatment or navigation model, not only color.
